From: bornaradusin <bornaradusin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:31:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 167/167] PC5: a range one block of direct memory can't back is
 backed with several; a refusal logs all the free memory

When the console's pool is split into pieces, a commit larger than its
largest free block was refused although enough memory was free, and the
game stopped (a Unity game: "Could not allocate memory: System out of
memory!" with 821 MiB free a minute before and a largest block of 52 MiB).
Such a range is now backed with several blocks, each mapped where the one
before ends, sized by the largest free block, at most 32. Only a refused
commit takes this path; one block still backs every range it can.

A refused call's line adds all the free memory of the pool, and the
title's minute line the largest free block, which together tell a full
pool from a fragmented one.
